
  Notifications are the last layer on that stack, not the first. Building them now would mean inventing all four along
  the way, which is how a "notifications" spec quietly becomes "build the whole app."

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether I'm designing notifications against a real app that lives somewhere I can't see, or the
  notifications idea is the entry point for building the tasks app itself.

  - [1mReal app exists elsewhere[0m — point me at it and this becomes a genuine notifications design: event sources, delivery
    channels, read state. Cheap, and the design will actually fit your data.
  - [1mThis repo is the whole app[0m — then the honest first project is the tasks app core (model + storage + UI), with
    notifications as a deliberate second spec. Slower to reach notifications, but each piece gets designed against
    something real.
  - [1mDesign notifications standalone anyway[0m — a self-contained spec with the task/user model stubbed as assumptions.
    Fastest to a document, but every interface in it is a guess, and guesses at interfaces are the expensive kind to
    redo.

  [1mMy recommendation: the second.[0m Given a one-commit repo with an empty page, I think this is greenfield and the
  decomposition is real work, not ceremony. But you know what's behind this and I don't.
